<edge source="us_china_strategic_rival::geopolitical_tension" target="ai_governance_and_race::regulatory_stringency" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">3</data>
  <data key="d36">0.75</data>
  <data key="d37">Rising strategic tensions lead governments to enact stricter AI export controls, security-driven licensing and emergency domestic restrictions to limit adversary access and protect critical systems.</data>
  <data key="d38">6-18 months</data>
  <data key="d39">us_china_strategic_rival</data>
  <data key="d40">ai_governance_and_race</data>
</edge>
<edge source="us_china_strategic_rival::geopolitical_tension" target="global_energy_supply_sho::chokepoint_congestion" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">3</data>
  <data key="d36">0.75</data>
  <data key="d37">Heightened US–China geopolitical tension drives increased naval patrols, convoy escorts, inspections and avoidance routing for tankers in key straits (e.g., Malacca, Hormuz, Suez), producing slower transits and localized bottlenecks at maritime chokepoints.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">us_china_strategic_rival</data>
  <data key="d40">global_energy_supply_sho</data>
</edge>
<edge source="us_china_strategic_rival::geopolitical_tension" target="global_energy_supply_sho::diesel_price_level" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">2</data>
  <data key="d36">0.65</data>
  <data key="d37">Rising geopolitical tension raises shipping risk premia and marine insurance costs and prompts precautionary buying and margin calls, which increase market risk pricing and push up global diesel benchmark levels even without an immediate physical shortage.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">us_china_strategic_rival</data>
  <data key="d40">global_energy_supply_sho</data>
</edge>
<edge source="us_china_strategic_rival::geopolitical_tension" target="persian_gulf_conflict::escalation_intensity" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">3</data>
  <data key="d36">0.8</data>
  <data key="d37">A rise in great-power geopolitical tension drives increased US and Chinese naval and air deployments plus amplified proxy support to regional actors, raising the frequency of close encounters, interdictions and miscalculations that directly increase operational escalation in the Gulf.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">us_china_strategic_rival</data>
  <data key="d40">persian_gulf_conflict</data>
</edge>
<edge source="us_china_strategic_rival::geopolitical_tension" target="persian_gulf_conflict::chokepoint_traffic_volum" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">2</data>
  <data key="d36">0.7</data>
  <data key="d37">Intensified US–China rivalry leads to more forward naval and air transits and freedom-of-navigation operations through Gulf chokepoints, raising aggregate military transit volume even as commercial actors may begin to adjust.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">us_china_strategic_rival</data>
  <data key="d40">persian_gulf_conflict</data>
</edge>
<edge source="us_china_strategic_rival::geopolitical_tension" target="russia_ukraine_war::defense_production_rate" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">-</data>
  <data key="d29">2</data>
  <data key="d36">0.6</data>
  <data key="d37">A rise in US–China geopolitical tension drives US and allied procurement authorities and prime contractors to reassign production slots and prioritize Indo‑Pacific capabilities, slowing the flow of new deliveries and procurements directed at Ukraine.</data>
  <data key="d38">6-18 months</data>
  <data key="d39">us_china_strategic_rival</data>
  <data key="d40">russia_ukraine_war</data>
</edge>
