<edge source="us_china_strategic_rival::diplomatic_engagement" target="persian_gulf_conflict::risk_premia_level" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">-</data>
  <data key="d29">2</data>
  <data key="d36">0.6</data>
  <data key="d37">Higher-frequency, high-level US–China diplomacy reduces uncertainty about crisis management and deconfliction, lowering market and insurer perceptions of systemic risk for Gulf routes and thus compressing risk premia; the reverse (less engagement) raises premia.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">us_china_strategic_rival</data>
  <data key="d40">persian_gulf_conflict</data>
</edge>
<edge source="us_china_strategic_rival::diplomatic_engagement" target="russia_ukraine_war::sanctions_enforcement" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">-</data>
  <data key="d29">2</data>
  <data key="d36">0.6</data>
  <data key="d37">Increased high‑level diplomatic engagement between Washington and Beijing produces bargaining over Russia policy and can result in China resisting or narrowing cooperation on secondary sanctions and information‑sharing, reducing overall sanctions enforcement pressure on Russian actors.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">us_china_strategic_rival</data>
  <data key="d40">russia_ukraine_war</data>
</edge>
